# Code style

- C++20, modern features where they help (designated initializers, `std::span`, `std::format`, ranges, RAII). No raw `new`/`delete`.
- Naming (enforced by `.clang-tidy`):
  - PascalCase: types, enum values, free functions, namespace-scope variables, `constexpr` constants (`ParseOptions`, `FramesInFlight`).
  - camelCase: methods, members, locals, parameters (`recordFrame`, `imageCount`).
  - Private/protected members: camelCase with a trailing underscore (`device_`).
  - Namespaces: short lower-case and only where they add meaning (`sph`, `sph::gpu`, `sph::render`).
  - Files: PascalCase named after their main type or function (`gpu/Context.hpp`), Slang modules too (`Background.slang`).
- Formatting: `.clang-format` (Google base, 100 columns). Run `clang-format -i` on touched files.
- Comments only where the code cannot speak for itself: invariants, Vulkan sync reasoning, non-obvious math. No narration, no change logs.
- Vulkan objects are owned by RAII types (`gpu::Unique`, `gpu::Buffer`, `gpu::Image`); declare members so destruction order is correct, nothing outlives `gpu::Context`.
- Errors: throw (`gpu::Check` for `VkResult`); catch only in `main` or where recovery is real.
- Vulkan 1.3 core only: dynamic rendering, synchronization2, buffer device addresses for shader data.
- Structs shared with shaders: keep C++ and Slang layouts identical and `static_assert` the size.
- Third-party headers are SYSTEM includes; our code builds warning-free with `SPH_WARNINGS_AS_ERRORS=ON` on GCC, Clang, and MSVC.
